  // moment): `Flip.from(flipState, {targets, ...})` captures its own targets' TRUE final resting
  // ("to") position by reading their live layout at the instant it's called — synchronously, not
  // lazily. The old code pinned the new entrant to `position:absolute` (removing it from this
  // row's flex flow) BEFORE calling Flip.from(), which meant Flip measured every EXISTING card's
  // resting position as if the new entrant weren't there at all — one card too few sharing the
  // row's flex space. Flip then spent the whole tween sliding existing cards toward that
  // subtly-wrong position, and the instant both groups reverted to static flow together (Cause D's
  // fix), the real flex layout — WITH the entrant correctly occupying its slot — recomputed and
  // every existing card visibly snapped from Flip's (wrong) target position to its true one.
  // Fix: apply the entrant's `position:absolute` pin (this function) only AFTER Flip.from() has
  // already been called below, using the rects measured above — while the entrant was still
  // genuinely in flow, so Flip's own targets correctly accounted for it taking up space. Applying
  // the already-computed pin afterward doesn't need to re-measure anything (the numbers were
