  // land at roughly the same moment Flip's own onComplete reverts ITS targets back to static flow.
  // "Roughly the same moment" turned out not to be good enough — a live trace showed that even a
  // small deliberate +0.02s buffer between the two was, on its own, enough to reproduce the exact
  // jump: during that gap, Flip's just-reverted targets compute their static flex position as if
  // the still-pinned new entrants don't exist yet, then the entrants themselves pop in a frame or
  // two later and everything reflows again — a visible double-jump. Defined once here (in scope
  // for both call sites below: synchronously inside Flip.from()'s own onComplete when gsapFlip is
  // true, so both groups revert in the exact same tick with zero gap; or via a plain delayedCall
  // at `dur` when gsapFlip is false and there's no concurrent Flip tween to synchronize with at
  // all). Guarded against double-invocation (e.g. a stray second call) by nulling newElRects out
  // once released.
